        <div class="lvn-narrative-body">
          <p>Diamonds are formed when pure carbon is subjected to extreme heat and pressure, causing it to crystallise into one of the hardest natural materials on earth. In nature, this process takes place deep beneath the earth's surface over millions of years through natural geological forces.</p>
          <p>In laboratory environments, these same conditions are carefully recreated using advanced technology, allowing a diamond crystal to grow in a controlled setting over a much shorter time. Although the timelines differ, both methods produce genuine diamonds with identical structure, durability, and brilliance.</p>
